#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721148 :question/problem "The vectors $\\vec{a}$ and $\\vec{b}$ are shown below. Each grid square has side length $1$.\n\n![Two vectors on a unit coordinate grid](images/d3/d367fde2b9a8820b314c93da2110593446835c89bfe499d1152b5534f359bfa3.png)\n\nFind the coordinates of $2\\vec{a}-3\\vec{b}$.\n\n$2\\vec{a}-3\\vec{b}=\\langle$ {{field-1}} $,\\,$ {{field-2}} $\\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "The arrowheads give the components because both arrows start at the origin:\n\n$$\n\\vec{a}=\\langle 3,2\\rangle,\\qquad \\vec{b}=\\langle -1,4\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFirst scale both vectors:\n\n$$\n2\\vec{a}=\\langle 6,4\\rangle,\\qquad 3\\vec{b}=\\langle -3,12\\rangle.\n$$\n\nNow subtract corresponding components. Subtracting the negative first component of $3\\vec{b}$ adds $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2\\vec{a}-3\\vec{b}&=\\langle 6,4\\rangle-\\langle -3,12\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 6-(-3),4-12\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 9,-8\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721154 :question/problem "If $\\vec{p}=\\langle -3,2\\rangle$, find $-2\\vec{p}$.\n\n$-2\\vec{p}=\\langle$ {{field-1}} $,\\,$ {{field-2}} $\\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "Multiply each component by $-2$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-2\\vec{p}&=-2\\langle -3,2\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle (-2)(-3),(-2)(2)\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle 6,-4\\rangle.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first component becomes positive because it is a product of two negative numbers. The second component becomes negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721171 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface generated by the vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\begin{pmatrix}- u v\\\\- u\\\\v\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n" :question/worked-solution "The fundamental vector product is $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$, in that order. Hold the other parameter constant in each partial derivative.\n\nDifferentiate the coordinates to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(- u v)&=- v\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(- u)&=-1\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(v)&=0\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(- u v)&=- u\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(- u)&=0\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(v)&=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent vectors are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u&=\\begin{pmatrix}- v\\\\-1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\\\\\mathbf r_v&=\\begin{pmatrix}- u\\\\0\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nForm the ordered determinant:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\- v & -1 & 0\\\\- u & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)_{1}&=(-1)(1)\\\\&\\quad -(0)(0)\\\\&=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)_{2}&=-(- v)(1)\\\\&\\quad +(0)(- u)\\\\&=v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)_{3}&=(- v)(0)\\\\&\\quad -(-1)(- u)\\\\&=- u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{pmatrix}-1\\\\v\\\\- u\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721173 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface generated by the vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\begin{pmatrix}u\\\\2 u - e^{v}\\\\v\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n" :question/worked-solution "The fundamental vector product is $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$, in that order. Hold the other parameter constant in each partial derivative.\n\nDifferentiate the coordinates to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(u)&=1\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(2 u - e^{v})&=2\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(v)&=0\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(u)&=0\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(2 u - e^{v})&=- e^{v}\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(v)&=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent vectors are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u&=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\2\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\\\\\mathbf r_v&=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\- e^{v}\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nForm the ordered determinant:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\1 & 2 & 0\\\\0 & - e^{v} & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)_{1}&=(2)(1)\\\\&\\quad -(0)(- e^{v})\\\\&=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)_{2}&=-(1)(1)\\\\&\\quad +(0)(0)\\\\&=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)_{3}&=(1)(- e^{v})\\\\&\\quad -(2)(0)\\\\&=- e^{v}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{pmatrix}2\\\\-1\\\\- e^{v}\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721177 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface generated by the vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\begin{pmatrix}- 2 u^{2} - v^{2}\\\\v\\\\u\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n" :question/worked-solution "The fundamental vector product is $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$, in that order. Hold the other parameter constant in each partial derivative.\n\nDifferentiate the coordinates to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(- 2 u^{2} - v^{2})&=- 4 u\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(v)&=0\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(u)&=1\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(- 2 u^{2} - v^{2})&=- 2 v\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(v)&=1\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(u)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent vectors are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u&=\\begin{pmatrix}- 4 u\\\\0\\\\1\\end{pmatrix},\\\\\\mathbf r_v&=\\begin{pmatrix}- 2 v\\\\1\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nForm the ordered determinant:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\- 4 u & 0 & 1\\\\- 2 v & 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)_{1}&=(0)(0)\\\\&\\quad -(1)(1)\\\\&=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)_{2}&=-(- 4 u)(0)\\\\&\\quad +(1)(- 2 v)\\\\&=- 2 v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)_{3}&=(- 4 u)(1)\\\\&\\quad -(0)(- 2 v)\\\\&=- 4 u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{pmatrix}-1\\\\- 2 v\\\\- 4 u\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721180 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface generated by the vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos{(u )}\\\\\\sin{(u )}\\\\v\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n" :question/worked-solution "The fundamental vector product is $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$, in that order. Hold the other parameter constant in each partial derivative.\n\nDifferentiate the coordinates to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(\\cos{(u )})&=- \\sin{(u )}\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(\\sin{(u )})&=\\cos{(u )}\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(v)&=0\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(\\cos{(u )})&=0\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(\\sin{(u )})&=0\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(v)&=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent vectors are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u&=\\begin{pmatrix}- \\sin{(u )}\\\\\\cos{(u )}\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\\\\\mathbf r_v&=\\begin{pmatrix}0\\\\0\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nForm the ordered determinant:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\- \\sin{(u )} & \\cos{(u )} & 0\\\\0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)_{1}&=(\\cos{(u )})(1)\\\\&\\quad -(0)(0)\\\\&=\\cos{(u )}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)_{2}&=-(- \\sin{(u )})(1)\\\\&\\quad +(0)(0)\\\\&=\\sin{(u )}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)_{3}&=(- \\sin{(u )})(0)\\\\&\\quad -(\\cos{(u )})(0)\\\\&=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos{(u )}\\\\\\sin{(u )}\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721198 :question/problem "Find the fundamental vector product of the surface generated by the vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\begin{pmatrix}v \\cos{(u )}\\\\\\sin{(u )}\\\\- v^{2}\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n" :question/worked-solution "The fundamental vector product is $\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v$, in that order. Hold the other parameter constant in each partial derivative.\n\nDifferentiate the coordinates to obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(v \\cos{(u )})&=- v \\sin{(u )}\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(\\sin{(u )})&=\\cos{(u )}\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}(- v^{2})&=0\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(v \\cos{(u )})&=\\cos{(u )}\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(\\sin{(u )})&=0\\\\\\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}(- v^{2})&=- 2 v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent vectors are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u&=\\begin{pmatrix}- v \\sin{(u )}\\\\\\cos{(u )}\\\\0\\end{pmatrix},\\\\\\mathbf r_v&=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos{(u )}\\\\0\\\\- 2 v\\end{pmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nForm the ordered determinant:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i&\\mathbf j&\\mathbf k\\\\- v \\sin{(u )} & \\cos{(u )} & 0\\\\\\cos{(u )} & 0 & - 2 v\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)_{1}&=(\\cos{(u )})(- 2 v)\\\\&\\quad -(0)(0)\\\\&=- 2 v \\cos{(u )}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)_{2}&=-(- v \\sin{(u )})(- 2 v)\\\\&\\quad +(0)(\\cos{(u )})\\\\&=- 2 v^{2} \\sin{(u )}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v)_{3}&=(- v \\sin{(u )})(0)\\\\&\\quad -(\\cos{(u )})(\\cos{(u )})\\\\&=- \\cos^{2}{(u )}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{pmatrix}- 2 v \\cos{(u )}\\\\- 2 v^{2} \\sin{(u )}\\\\- \\cos^{2}{(u )}\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721317 :question/problem "The path $C$ follows the Cartesian curve $y^3=x+1$ from $A(-1,0)$ to $B(0,1)$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C xy\\,\\mathrm{d}x+(x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Rearrange the curve equation as $x=y^3-1$. Choosing $y$ as the parameter gives\n\n$$\nx(t)=t^3-1,\\qquad y(t)=t,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1.\n$$\n\nAt $t=0$ the point is $(-1,0)$, and at $t=1$ it is $(0,1)$, so this parametrization has the required direction. Its derivatives are\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}=3t^2,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}=1.\n$$\n\nWrite the given integral as $I$. Substituting both coordinates into both coefficients and retaining the derivative factors gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI\n&=\\int_0^1\\bigl((t^3-1)t\\cdot 3t^2+(t^3-1+t)\\cdot 1\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1(3t^6-2t^3+t-1)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{3t^7}{7}-\\frac{t^4}{2}+\\frac{t^2}{2}-t\\right]_0^1\\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{7}-\\frac12+\\frac12-1\\\\\n&=-\\frac47.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721324 :question/problem "The path $C$ follows the parabola $y=x^2-x$ from $A(0,0)$ to $B(2,2)$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C xy\\,\\mathrm{d}x+x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Since $y$ is given as a function of $x$, choose\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\qquad y(t)=t^2-t,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 2.\n$$\n\nThe endpoints are reached in the stated order. Also,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}=1,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}=2t-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C xy\\,\\mathrm{d}x+x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n&=\\int_0^2\\bigl(t(t^2-t)\\cdot 1+t(2t-1)\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_0^2(t^3+t^2-t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{t^4}{4}+\\frac{t^3}{3}-\\frac{t^2}{2}\\right]_0^2\\\\\n&=4+\\frac{8}{3}-2\\\\\n&=\\frac{14}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721327 :question/problem "The path $C$ follows $y=x^2-x$ from $A(0,0)$ to $B(2,2)$. Consider\n\n$$\n\\int_C y\\,\\mathrm{d}x+x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$\n\n*Hint: Use $x(t)=t$ and $y(t)=t^2-t$.*\n\nUsing this parametrization, which definite integral is equivalent to the line integral?" :question/worked-solution "The endpoint $x$-coordinates give $0\\le t\\le 2$. Differentiating the supplied parametrization gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}t}=1,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}t}=2t-1.\n$$\n\nSubstitute into each coefficient and multiply by its corresponding derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C y\\,\\mathrm{d}x+x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n&=\\int_0^2\\bigl((t^2-t)\\cdot 1+t(2t-1)\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_0^2(3t^2-2t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the requested definite integral; no evaluation is required."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721371 :question/problem "$$\n\\iint_D\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\,\\mathrm dA\n$$\n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 1,\\ 0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u,\\,v,\\,2 u^{2} + 3 v^{2}\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D$.\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The surface-area integrand is the magnitude of the fundamental vector product:\n\n$$\nA(S)=\\iint_D \\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate with respect to each parameter:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u&=\\langle 1,\\,0,\\,4 u\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v&=\\langle 0,\\,1,\\,6 v\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpand the determinant to find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i & \\mathbf j & \\mathbf k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 u \\\\ 0 & 1 & 6 v\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle - 4 u,\\,- 6 v,\\,1\\rangle\n$$\n\nSquare the components, add, and take the nonnegative square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|^2&=\\left(- 4 u\\right)^2+\\left(- 6 v\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(1\\right)^2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|&=\\sqrt{16 u^{2} + 36 v^{2} + 1} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{16 u^{2} + 36 v^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the required integral is\n\n$$\nA(S)=\\iint_D \\sqrt{16 u^{2} + 36 v^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $\\sqrt{16 u^{2} + 36 v^{2} + 1}$. The integral does not need to be evaluated."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721377 :question/problem "$$\n\\iint_D\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\,\\mathrm dA\n$$\n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 2,\\ 0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle 4 u^{2},\\,u - v^{2},\\,v^{2}\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D$.\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The surface-area integrand is the magnitude of the fundamental vector product:\n\n$$\nA(S)=\\iint_D \\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate with respect to each parameter:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u&=\\langle 8 u,\\,1,\\,0\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v&=\\langle 0,\\,- 2 v,\\,2 v\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpand the determinant to find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i & \\mathbf j & \\mathbf k \\\\ 8 u & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & - 2 v & 2 v\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle 2 v,\\,- 16 u v,\\,- 16 u v\\rangle\n$$\n\nSquare the components, add, and take the nonnegative square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|^2&=\\left(2 v\\right)^2+\\left(- 16 u v\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(- 16 u v\\right)^2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|&=\\sqrt{512 u^{2} v^{2} + 4 v^{2}} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{4 v^{2} \\left(128 u^{2} + 1\\right)} \\\\ &=2 v \\sqrt{128 u^{2} + 1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn $D$, both parameters are nonnegative, so $\\sqrt{v^2}=|v|=v$.\n\nThus the required integral is\n\n$$\nA(S)=\\iint_D 2 v \\sqrt{128 u^{2} + 1}\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $2 v \\sqrt{128 u^{2} + 1}$. The integral does not need to be evaluated."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721383 :question/problem "$$\n\\iint_D\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\,\\mathrm dA\n$$\n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 2,\\ 0\\le v\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u,\\,v,\\,3 u + 4 v\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D$.\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The surface-area integrand is the magnitude of the fundamental vector product:\n\n$$\nA(S)=\\iint_D \\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate with respect to each parameter:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u&=\\langle 1,\\,0,\\,3\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v&=\\langle 0,\\,1,\\,4\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpand the determinant to find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i & \\mathbf j & \\mathbf k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle -3,\\,-4,\\,1\\rangle\n$$\n\nSquare the components, add, and take the nonnegative square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|^2&=\\left(-3\\right)^2+\\left(-4\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(1\\right)^2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|&=\\sqrt{26} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{26}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the required integral is\n\n$$\nA(S)=\\iint_D \\sqrt{26}\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $\\sqrt{26}$. The integral does not need to be evaluated."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721385 :question/problem "$$\n\\iint_D\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\,\\mathrm dA\n$$\n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 1,\\ 0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle v,\\,u,\\,4 u + 5 v\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D$.\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The surface-area integrand is the magnitude of the fundamental vector product:\n\n$$\nA(S)=\\iint_D \\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate with respect to each parameter:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u&=\\langle 0,\\,1,\\,4\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v&=\\langle 1,\\,0,\\,5\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpand the determinant to find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i & \\mathbf j & \\mathbf k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 5\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle 5,\\,4,\\,-1\\rangle\n$$\n\nSquare the components, add, and take the nonnegative square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|^2&=\\left(5\\right)^2+\\left(4\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(-1\\right)^2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|&=\\sqrt{42} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{42}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the required integral is\n\n$$\nA(S)=\\iint_D \\sqrt{42}\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $\\sqrt{42}$. The integral does not need to be evaluated."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721401 :question/problem "$$\n\\iint_D\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\,\\mathrm dA\n$$\n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 2,\\ 0\\le v\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u + v,\\,3 - u,\\,11 u + 8 v + 1\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D$.\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The surface-area integrand is the magnitude of the fundamental vector product:\n\n$$\nA(S)=\\iint_D \\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate with respect to each parameter:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u&=\\langle 1,\\,-1,\\,11\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v&=\\langle 1,\\,0,\\,8\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpand the determinant to find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i & \\mathbf j & \\mathbf k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 11 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 8\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle -8,\\,3,\\,1\\rangle\n$$\n\nSquare the components, add, and take the nonnegative square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|^2&=\\left(-8\\right)^2+\\left(3\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(1\\right)^2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|&=\\sqrt{74} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{74}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the required integral is\n\n$$\nA(S)=\\iint_D \\sqrt{74}\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $\\sqrt{74}$. The integral does not need to be evaluated."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721407 :question/problem "$$\n\\iint_D\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\,\\mathrm dA\n$$\n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 1,\\ 0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle v,\\,u,\\,3 u + 3 v\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D$.\n\nThe tangent vectors are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u&=\\langle 0,\\,1,\\,3\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v&=\\langle 1,\\,0,\\,3\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The surface-area integrand is the magnitude of the fundamental vector product:\n\n$$\nA(S)=\\iint_D \\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate with respect to each parameter:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u&=\\langle 0,\\,1,\\,3\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v&=\\langle 1,\\,0,\\,3\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpand the determinant to find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i & \\mathbf j & \\mathbf k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle 3,\\,3,\\,-1\\rangle\n$$\n\nSquare the components, add, and take the nonnegative square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|^2&=\\left(3\\right)^2+\\left(3\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(-1\\right)^2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|&=\\sqrt{19} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{19}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the required integral is\n\n$$\nA(S)=\\iint_D \\sqrt{19}\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $\\sqrt{19}$. The integral does not need to be evaluated."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721408 :question/problem "$$\n\\iint_D\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\,\\mathrm dA\n$$\n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 1,\\ 0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle v,\\,u,\\,2 u + v\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D$.\n\nThe tangent vectors are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u&=\\langle 0,\\,1,\\,2\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v&=\\langle 1,\\,0,\\,1\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The surface-area integrand is the magnitude of the fundamental vector product:\n\n$$\nA(S)=\\iint_D \\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate with respect to each parameter:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u&=\\langle 0,\\,1,\\,2\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v&=\\langle 1,\\,0,\\,1\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpand the determinant to find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i & \\mathbf j & \\mathbf k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle 1,\\,2,\\,-1\\rangle\n$$\n\nSquare the components, add, and take the nonnegative square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|^2&=\\left(1\\right)^2+\\left(2\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(-1\\right)^2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|&=\\sqrt{6} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the required integral is\n\n$$\nA(S)=\\iint_D \\sqrt{6}\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $\\sqrt{6}$. The integral does not need to be evaluated."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721421 :question/problem "$$\n\\iint_D\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\,\\mathrm dA\n$$\n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 1,\\ 0\\le v\\le 2\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle v,\\,u,\\,5 u + 3 v\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D$.\n\nThe tangent vectors are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u&=\\langle 0,\\,1,\\,5\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v&=\\langle 1,\\,0,\\,3\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The surface-area integrand is the magnitude of the fundamental vector product:\n\n$$\nA(S)=\\iint_D \\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate with respect to each parameter:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u&=\\langle 0,\\,1,\\,5\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v&=\\langle 1,\\,0,\\,3\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpand the determinant to find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i & \\mathbf j & \\mathbf k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle 3,\\,5,\\,-1\\rangle\n$$\n\nSquare the components, add, and take the nonnegative square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|^2&=\\left(3\\right)^2+\\left(5\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(-1\\right)^2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|&=\\sqrt{35} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{35}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the required integral is\n\n$$\nA(S)=\\iint_D \\sqrt{35}\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $\\sqrt{35}$. The integral does not need to be evaluated."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721430 :question/problem "$$\n\\iint_D\\boxed{\\phantom{AAAAAAAA}}\\,\\mathrm dA\n$$\n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v):0\\le u\\le 2,\\ 0\\le v\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nThe integral above gives the surface area of the parametric surface\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(u,v)=\\langle u,\\,v,\\,3 u + 2 v\\rangle\n$$\n\nfor $(u,v)\\in D$.\n\nThe tangent vectors are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u&=\\langle 1,\\,0,\\,3\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v&=\\langle 0,\\,1,\\,2\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "The surface-area integrand is the magnitude of the fundamental vector product:\n\n$$\nA(S)=\\iint_D \\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each coordinate with respect to each parameter:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf r_u&=\\langle 1,\\,0,\\,3\\rangle \\\\ \\mathbf r_v&=\\langle 0,\\,1,\\,2\\rangle\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpand the determinant to find the cross product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\begin{vmatrix}\\mathbf i & \\mathbf j & \\mathbf k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{vmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v=\\langle -3,\\,-2,\\,1\\rangle\n$$\n\nSquare the components, add, and take the nonnegative square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|^2&=\\left(-3\\right)^2+\\left(-2\\right)^2 \\\\ &\\quad+\\left(1\\right)^2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\|\\mathbf r_u\\times\\mathbf r_v\\right\\|&=\\sqrt{14} \\\\ &=\\sqrt{14}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus the required integral is\n\n$$\nA(S)=\\iint_D \\sqrt{14}\\,\\mathrm dA.\n$$\n\nThe missing expression is $\\sqrt{14}$. The integral does not need to be evaluated."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721433 :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(2,1)$ to $B(0,5)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Use the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\langle1+t,3-2t\\rangle,\n$$\n\nwith parameter bounds chosen to match this traversal, to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C xy\\,\\mathrm{d}x+x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First match the parameter to the ordered endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(1)=\\langle2,1\\rangle=A,\\qquad\n\\mathbf{r}(-1)=\\langle0,5\\rangle=B.\n$$\n\nThe traversal from $A$ to $B$ therefore takes $t$ from $1$ to $-1$. Increasing $t$ from $-1$ to $1$ would traverse the segment in the opposite direction.\n\nHere $x(t)=1+t$, $y(t)=3-2t$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle1,-2\\rangle$, giving $\\mathrm{d}x=\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=-2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$. Use these derivatives with the bounds just found:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C xy\\,\\mathrm{d}x+x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n&=\\int_1^{-1}\\bigl(x(t)y(t)x'(t)+x(t)y'(t)\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_1^{-1}\\bigl((1+t)(3-2t)-2(1+t)\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_1^{-1}(1-t-2t^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[t-\\frac12t^2-\\frac23t^3\\right]_1^{-1}\\\\\n&=\\left(-1-\\frac12+\\frac23\\right)-\\left(1-\\frac12-\\frac23\\right)\\\\\n&=-\\frac56-\\left(-\\frac16\\right)\\\\\n&=-\\frac23.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe decreasing parameter bounds already enforce the stated direction; no additional sign change is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721437 :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(1,2)$ to $B(3,1)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C xy\\,\\mathrm{d}x+x\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The endpoint parametrization is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a})\\\\\n&=\\langle1,2\\rangle+t\\langle2,-1\\rangle\\\\\n&=\\langle1+2t,2-t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence $x(t)=1+2t$, $y(t)=2-t$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle2,-1\\rangle$. In particular, $\\mathrm{d}x=2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=-\\mathrm{d}t$; the negative sign records the decreasing $y$-coordinate.\n\nNow substitute both coordinates in the product $xy$, retaining the signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C xy\\,\\mathrm{d}x+x\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n&=\\int_0^1\\bigl(x(t)y(t)x'(t)+x(t)y'(t)\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1\\bigl(2(1+2t)(2-t)-(1+2t)\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1(3+4t-4t^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[3t+2t^2-\\frac43t^3\\right]_0^1\\\\\n&=3+2-\\frac43\\\\\n&=\\frac{11}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721450 :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(0,0)$ to $B(2,1)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C x\\,\\mathrm{d}x+y\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a})$, with $0\\le t\\le1$. Here,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\langle 2t,t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThus $x(t)=2t$, $y(t)=t$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle2,1\\rangle$, so $\\mathrm{d}x=2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nSubstitute each coordinate and its corresponding differential:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C x\\,\\mathrm{d}x+y\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n&=\\int_0^1\\bigl(x(t)x'(t)+y(t)y'(t)\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1\\bigl((2t)(2)+t(1)\\bigr)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\int_0^1 5t\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac52t^2\\right]_0^1\\\\\n&=\\frac52.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721454 :question/problem "![](images/47/4727c988042bd8d63af370c507db8988b3024cf7e75c86d40bc70cdcfd744873.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C(x+2y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of $x^2+y^2=4$ traversed counterclockwise from $(0,2)$ to $(-2,0)$ in the second quadrant, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))x'(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe circle has radius $2$. Choose\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle2\\cos t,2\\sin t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nIncreasing $t$ moves counterclockwise. The starting point $(0,2)$ corresponds to $t=\\pi/2$, and the ending point $(-2,0)$ corresponds to $t=\\pi$. This interval traces exactly the indicated arc.\n\nNow compute the two factors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(\\mathbf r(t))&=2\\cos t+4\\sin t=2(\\cos t+2\\sin t),\\\\\nx'(t)&=-2\\sin t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C(x+2y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}2(\\cos t+2\\sin t)(-2\\sin t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=-4\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}(\\cos t+2\\sin t)\\sin t\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x$ decreases along the arc, so the negative sign in $x'(t)$ is essential. Using $y'(t)=2\\cos t$ would construct a different differential."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721467 :question/problem "![](images/43/43c8d1ced440e0fe79052c1329ff3af865e1fdbfb4cc0c7ad0a9c176592358e9.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C (x+2y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment traversed from $(-1,1)$ to $(2,2)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral.\n\n*Hint: Use $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3t-1,t+1\\rangle$.*" :question/worked-solution "Use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))x'(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe supplied parametrization starts at $(-1,1)$ when $t=0$ and ends at $(2,2)$ when $t=1$. Thus the limits are $0$ and $1$.\n\nDifferentiate the $x$-coordinate:\n\n$$\nx'(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3t-1)=3.\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into the function:\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=(3t-1)+2(t+1)=5t+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C(x+2y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\int_0^1(5t+1)\\cdot3\\,\\mathrm{d}t=3\\int_0^1(5t+1)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe factor $3$ comes from $\\mathrm{d}x=3\\,\\mathrm{d}t$; substituting into the function alone omits it."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721469 :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f01c4633489a913d93f86ccc83580963ea5cd41bed31347108730a7d60c3ebf.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x^2y\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the left half of the ellipse $3x^2+y^2=3$, traversed counterclockwise from $(0,\\sqrt{3})$ to $(0,-\\sqrt{3})$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))x'(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst put the ellipse in standard form:\n\n$$\nx^2+\\frac{y^2}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nIts horizontal semiaxis is $1$ and its vertical semiaxis is $\\sqrt{3}$. Choose\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\cos t,\\sqrt{3}\\sin t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe start, leftmost point, and finish occur at $t=\\pi/2$, $t=\\pi$, and $t=3\\pi/2$, respectively. Thus $\\pi/2\\le t\\le3\\pi/2$ traces the specified left half in the required direction.\n\nSubstitute and differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(\\mathbf r(t))&=(\\cos t)^2(\\sqrt{3}\\sin t)=\\sqrt{3}\\cos^2t\\sin t,\\\\\nx'(t)&=-\\sin t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C x^2y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\int_{\\pi/2}^{3\\pi/2}\\sqrt{3}\\cos^2t\\sin t(-\\sin t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=-\\sqrt{3}\\int_{\\pi/2}^{3\\pi/2}\\cos^2t\\sin^2t\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlthough $x$ returns to $0$, the integral need not vanish: $x$ decreases on the upper half and increases on the lower half, while $y$ changes sign. The product $x^2y\\,x'(t)$ is nonpositive on both halves. Retain the signed derivative rather than replacing it with speed or $|x'(t)|$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721544 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{u}=⟨2,-9⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{v}=⟨4,6⟩$, then $\\overset{\\to }{u}+\\overset{\\to }{v}=$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{u}+\\overset{\\to }{v} & =⟨2,-9⟩+⟨4,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2+4,-9+6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨6,-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721550 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{u} = ⟨4, 1⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{v} = ⟨-9,2⟩$, then $\\overset{\\to }{u} - \\overset{\\to }{v} =$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{u} - \\overset{\\to }{v} &= ⟨4,1⟩ - ⟨-9,2⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨4-(-9),1-2⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨13,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721553 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{m} = ⟨6, -2⟩$ and $\\overset{\\to }{n} = ⟨1,-1⟩$, then\n\n$\\overset{\\to }{m} - \\overset{\\to }{n} =$ $\\langle$ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $\\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{m} - \\overset{\\to }{n} &= ⟨6,-2⟩ - ⟨1,-1⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨6-1,-2-(-1)⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨5,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721556 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{p} = ⟨7,-1⟩$, then\n\n$-6\\overset{\\to }{p} =$ $\\langle$ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $\\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6\\overset{\\to }{p} &= (-6)\\cdot ⟨7,-1⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨(-6)\\cdot 7,(-6)\\cdot (-1)⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨-42,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721558 :question/problem "If $\\overset{\\to }{p} = ⟨5,-3⟩$, then\n\n$4\\overset{\\to }{p} =$ $\\langle$ {{field-1}} $,$ {{field-2}} $\\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the result, we just need to perform the required operations with each component separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\overset{\\to }{p} &= 4\\cdot ⟨5,-3⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨4\\cdot 5,4\\cdot (-3)⟩ \\\\ &= ⟨20,-12⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721586 :question/problem "![Directed path C](images/fa/fa0ea1d3a658c7da952caa60d9a29e19995c5f9aefe138b13dee7ea42c67ab58.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C(x^2+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the upper-left quarter of the ellipse $x^2+4y^2=4$, directed counterclockwise from $A=(0,1)$ to $B=(-2,0)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle x(t),y(t)\\rangle$ that follows the directed path,\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))\\,y'(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWriting the ellipse as $x^2/4+y^2=1$ gives the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle2\\cos t,\\sin t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe initial point $(0,1)$ occurs at $t=\\pi/2$, and the terminal point $(-2,0)$ occurs at $t=\\pi$. Increasing $t$ through this interval follows the upper-left arc counterclockwise. Thus\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le t\\le\\pi,\\qquad y'(t)=\\cos t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into $f(x,y)=x^2+y$ gives\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=(2\\cos t)^2+\\sin t=4\\cos^2 t+\\sin t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C(x^2+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n=\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}(4\\cos^2 t+\\sin t)\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe factor $\\cos t$ retains its sign: $y$ decreases along this arc."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721598 :question/problem "![Directed path C](images/41/416b802710051fa30c2da3f637a8f94fccf8365a39144b6c82553eaad0de40a8.png)\n\nWhich expression is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(x+y+1)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the complete ellipse $x^2/4+y^2/9=1$, traversed once clockwise starting at $A=(0,3)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle x(t),y(t)\\rangle$ that follows the directed path,\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))\\,y'(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nChoose a parametrization that begins at the top of the ellipse and initially moves to the right:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle2\\sin t,3\\cos t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le2\\pi.\n$$\n\nIt lies on the ellipse because $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$. At $t=0$ the point is $(0,3)$; at $t=\\pi/2$ it is $(2,0)$. The path therefore runs clockwise, and the interval $[0,2\\pi]$ covers it exactly once.\n\nThe differential contributes\n\n$$\ny'(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3\\cos t)=-3\\sin t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ gives\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=2\\sin t+3\\cos t+1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these expressions, the requested line integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{2\\pi}(2\\sin t+3\\cos t+1)(-3\\sin t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFactoring out $-3$ gives the equivalent definite integral\n\n$$\n-3\\int_0^{2\\pi}(2\\sin t+3\\cos t+1)\\sin t\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlthough $C$ is closed, the integrand is not unity. The initial and terminal points alone do not determine this integral."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721602 :question/problem "![Directed path C](images/e7/e7b1742fad3cea5fb12d4a2e799aae284341f4c818a098f9c4395dbb340fe910.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the line segment directed from $A=(-1,-1)$ to $B=(2,1)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral.\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3t-1,2t-1\\rangle$.*" :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle x(t),y(t)\\rangle$ that follows the directed path,\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))\\,y'(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe hinted parametrization has $\\mathbf r(0)=(-1,-1)$ and $\\mathbf r(1)=(2,1)$, so it follows $C$ for $0\\le t\\le1$. Its $y$ coordinate gives\n\n$$\ny'(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2t-1)=2.\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into $f(x,y)=x+y$:\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=(3t-1)+(2t-1)=5t-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C(x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n&=\\int_0^1(5t-2)\\cdot2\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=2\\int_0^1(5t-2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721606 :question/problem "The vectors $\\overset{\\to }{a}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{b}$ are shown below. Each grid square has side length $1$.\n\n![Two vectors on a unit coordinate grid](images/2a/2af567064376869502ff35c96442d80bb92d0ee46deaf41514fdcbd71afca41f.png)\n\nFind the coordinates of $-2\\overset{\\to }{a}+\\overset{\\to }{b}$." :question/worked-solution "Both arrows start at the origin. Read their signed horizontal and vertical components:\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{a}=⟨1,4⟩,\\qquad \\overset{\\to }{b}=⟨-3,4⟩.\n$$\n\nScale each required vector, then combine corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\overset{\\to }{a}+\\overset{\\to }{b} &=(-2)\\cdot ⟨1,4⟩+1\\cdot ⟨-3,4⟩ \\\\ &=⟨-2,-8⟩+⟨-3,4⟩ \\\\ &=⟨-2+(-3),-8+4⟩ \\\\ &=⟨-5,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721612 :question/problem "The vectors $\\overset{\\to }{a}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{b}$ are shown below. Each grid square has side length $1$.\n\n![Two vectors on a unit coordinate grid](images/90/909ddd4e0fc719bf71686ef44dc01c200ce56227cdb4081822596fdc37309ae8.png)\n\nFind the coordinates of $\\overset{\\to }{a}-2\\overset{\\to }{b}$." :question/worked-solution "Both arrows start at the origin. Read their signed horizontal and vertical components:\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{a}=⟨4,-2⟩,\\qquad \\overset{\\to }{b}=⟨3,3⟩.\n$$\n\nScale each required vector, then combine corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{a}-2\\overset{\\to }{b} &=1\\cdot ⟨4,-2⟩+(-2)\\cdot ⟨3,3⟩ \\\\ &=⟨4,-2⟩+⟨-6,-6⟩ \\\\ &=⟨4+(-6),-2+(-6)⟩ \\\\ &=⟨-2,-8⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721617 :question/problem "The vectors $\\overset{\\to }{a}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{b}$ are shown below. Each grid square has side length $1$.\n\n![Two vectors on a unit coordinate grid](images/ef/ef308cba41adc2129841f74c7e265fb107fb99f719b15b6d0971fd33c436a818.png)\n\nFind the coordinates of $-3\\overset{\\to }{a}+\\overset{\\to }{b}$." :question/worked-solution "Both arrows start at the origin. Read their signed horizontal and vertical components:\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{a}=⟨1,-4⟩,\\qquad \\overset{\\to }{b}=⟨4,2⟩.\n$$\n\nScale each required vector, then combine corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\overset{\\to }{a}+\\overset{\\to }{b} &=(-3)\\cdot ⟨1,-4⟩+1\\cdot ⟨4,2⟩ \\\\ &=⟨-3,12⟩+⟨4,2⟩ \\\\ &=⟨-3+4,12+2⟩ \\\\ &=⟨1,14⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721630 :question/problem "The vectors $\\overset{\\to }{a}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{b}$ are shown below. Each grid square has side length $1$.\n\n![Two vectors on a unit coordinate grid](images/d4/d4401ba0dae26ae716de356c2220558f53a9321be9901ac41478cf6116c3445a.png)\n\nFind the coordinates of $2\\overset{\\to }{a}+\\overset{\\to }{b}$." :question/worked-solution "Both arrows start at the origin. Read their signed horizontal and vertical components:\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{a}=⟨-3,2⟩,\\qquad \\overset{\\to }{b}=⟨4,0⟩.\n$$\n\nScale each required vector, then combine corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\overset{\\to }{a}+\\overset{\\to }{b} &=2\\cdot ⟨-3,2⟩+1\\cdot ⟨4,0⟩ \\\\ &=⟨-6,4⟩+⟨4,0⟩ \\\\ &=⟨-6+4,4+0⟩ \\\\ &=⟨-2,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721643 :question/problem "The vectors $\\overset{\\to }{a}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{b}$ are shown below. Each grid square has side length $1$.\n\n![Two vectors on a unit coordinate grid](images/d9/d9427fc9f23dc15f371cec6ba371dc65a0fa26233fae91411fe65a710efc7861.png)\n\nFind the coordinates of $2\\overset{\\to }{a}-3\\overset{\\to }{b}$.\n\n$2\\overset{\\to }{a}-3\\overset{\\to }{b}=\\langle$ {{field-1}} $,\\,$ {{field-2}} $\\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "Both arrows start at the origin. Read their signed horizontal and vertical components:\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{a}=⟨2,-3⟩,\\qquad \\overset{\\to }{b}=⟨0,-3⟩.\n$$\n\nScale each required vector, then combine corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\overset{\\to }{a}-3\\overset{\\to }{b} &=2\\cdot ⟨2,-3⟩+(-3)\\cdot ⟨0,-3⟩ \\\\ &=⟨4,-6⟩+⟨0,9⟩ \\\\ &=⟨4+0,-6+9⟩ \\\\ &=⟨4,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721647 :question/problem "The vectors $\\overset{\\to }{a}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{b}$ are shown below. Each grid square has side length $1$.\n\n![Two vectors on a unit coordinate grid](images/20/20e42e54930a1b97f91517ebe4567ecd36ba9b64894052874354e34d9963541b.png)\n\nFind the coordinates of $2\\overset{\\to }{a}-3\\overset{\\to }{b}$.\n\n$2\\overset{\\to }{a}-3\\overset{\\to }{b}=\\langle$ {{field-1}} $,\\,$ {{field-2}} $\\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "Both arrows start at the origin. Read their signed horizontal and vertical components:\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{a}=⟨-2,0⟩,\\qquad \\overset{\\to }{b}=⟨1,2⟩.\n$$\n\nScale each required vector, then combine corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\overset{\\to }{a}-3\\overset{\\to }{b} &=2\\cdot ⟨-2,0⟩+(-3)\\cdot ⟨1,2⟩ \\\\ &=⟨-4,0⟩+⟨-3,-6⟩ \\\\ &=⟨-4+(-3),0+(-6)⟩ \\\\ &=⟨-7,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721654 :question/problem "The vectors $\\overset{\\to }{a}$ and $\\overset{\\to }{b}$ are shown below. Each grid square has side length $1$.\n\n![Two vectors on a unit coordinate grid](images/18/18561dbc8c4808f206e6ad73ce91a98c8f4c298f1a0b84e806e36cbd8b208360.png)\n\nFind the coordinates of $2\\overset{\\to }{a}+3\\overset{\\to }{b}$.\n\n$2\\overset{\\to }{a}+3\\overset{\\to }{b}=\\langle$ {{field-1}} $,\\,$ {{field-2}} $\\rangle$." :question/worked-solution "Both arrows start at the origin. Read their signed horizontal and vertical components:\n\n$$\n\\overset{\\to }{a}=⟨0,-3⟩,\\qquad \\overset{\\to }{b}=⟨1,3⟩.\n$$\n\nScale each required vector, then combine corresponding components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\overset{\\to }{a}+3\\overset{\\to }{b} &=2\\cdot ⟨0,-3⟩+3\\cdot ⟨1,3⟩ \\\\ &=⟨0,-6⟩+⟨3,9⟩ \\\\ &=⟨0+3,-6+9⟩ \\\\ &=⟨3,3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
